# v1.13 P4 PR review — the endorsement pass (last phase before the v1.13.0 release)

You are reviewing a PR to the Advisory Board conductor — a provider-agnostic CLI convening multiple frontier-model seats. The v1.13 milestone ("the board hands back a fixed copy") shipped a revision seat + `changes.json` (P2) and redline/patch/snippet rendering (P3). This PR is P4, the integrity capstone: the ENDORSEMENT PASS. It is the last code before v1.13.0 tags — review it as a release gate. SHIP = mergeable as-is; CAUTION/BLOCK must name the defect with file:line from the diff.

## The contract (decision D13, settled by the design roundtable)
Endorsement is ON by default for every `--output revised-draft` run (`--no-endorse` is the token-cost opt-out; un-endorsed output is "findings-mapped", only an endorsed run may say "board-endorsed"). After the revision passes its mechanical checks, each NON-revision seat gets ONE spawn, all fanned out CONCURRENTLY (the rounds ThreadPoolExecutor; wall-clock ≈ one extra round). Reply = one ENDORSE/OBJECT/ABSTAIN per edit AND per `unresolved` entry (a seat may object to how a conflict was characterized), optional note (required on OBJECT). The conductor builds rows `{seat, edit_n | unresolved_n, position, note?}` into `changes.json.endorsements` — models produce TOKENS, never rows; a failed/unparseable seat (after the two-attempt retry) records ABSTAIN rows with `dropped: true` and the reason in `note`; the pass NEVER fails the run, discards the revision, or moves exit codes; objections are recorded, never resolved (no discussion loop — the human decides, D6).

## The load-bearing constraint to check hardest
`verdict.json.changes` sha-pins the `changes.json` BYTES. The implementation restructured `_run_revision_step`: revision → checks → anti-smuggling guard (model-authored endorsements refused BEFORE the merge) → endorsement fan-out → rows merged into the changes dict + re-validated → draft written → changes.json written ONCE (rows inside) → pointer written ONCE from the same bytes. Any path where the pointer sha and the on-disk changes.json can diverge — or where a `--no-endorse` run's `changes.json` is not byte-identical to the P2/P3 shape (`endorsements: []`) — is a blocker.

## Also in the diff
- Template `advisory-board/endorsement@1` + sha in the recipe, printed at spawn; DATA fences + the P2 neutralizer; packet material = original source + revised draft + edits/unresolved tables (all already seen by every seat's provider in rounds — no new egress category; disclosure purpose mention).
- Row validation: the changes@1 validator additively extended — exactly one of `edit_n`/`unresolved_n` (1-based, in-range checked by the conductor), `position` enum, `dropped` strictly true-only, unknown keys refused. `seat` = the run's unique seat id (an id-vs-provider-name bug on duplicate-provider boards — `--board claude,claude,codex` — was found and fixed during implementation; check the fix is complete: exclusion, rows, artifacts, prompts all on the id axis).
- Artifacts: `endorsement/<seat>.md` + `.raw`, `prompts/endorsement-<seat>.prompt`; run card mentions the pass + seat count (a pre-spawn projection, name-axis by documented convention).
- Renderer: `{{ENDORSEMENT_SUMMARY}}` RAW token in the redline/patch section header — per-edit tally + objection notes (HTML-escaped); byte-identity for endorsement-less runs test-enforced.
- Recipe: resolved `endorse` bool + template sha persisted on revised-draft runs; `--from-recipe` replays validate; `--no-endorse` refused without `--output revised-draft`.
- Docs: SKILL.md, scripts/README.md, changes-schema.md (the P4 fill its P2 text promised), CHANGELOG.

## Standing invariants (violations are defects)
No-flags default run byte-identical; single-seat board (revision seat is the only seat) → zero endorsement seats → empty rows + note, not a crash; stdlib-only; no unseeded clocks; hand-authored changes.json/verdict.json must never produce raw tracebacks (clean exit 2); old artifacts (P2/P3 changes.json with empty endorsements) validate and render unchanged.

## What to scrutinize hardest
1. The write order + pointer sha under every branch: endorsement ON/OFF, all-seats-dropped, partial drops, the anti-smuggling guard path — can changes.json on disk EVER differ from the pointer's pinned bytes?
2. Parse robustness: the JSON positions array (missing target, duplicate target, extra target, garbage, marker echo) — every malformed shape must classify invalid → retry → dropped-ABSTAIN, never a partial row set for that seat.
3. The id-axis fix completeness (duplicate-provider boards): exclusion of the revision seat, row attribution, artifact filenames, prompt filenames, and the record when two seats share a provider.
4. Concurrency: the fan-out mutating shared state (rows list, warning flags) — thread-safety of what each worker touches; deterministic row ORDER in changes.json (byte-stability across runs with the same replies).
5. Renderer: escaping of objection notes into HTML; the RAW token's empty-drop rule (no blank residue); byte-identity for endorsement-less runs.
6. Validator: type-confusion matrix on the new row fields; can a row reference an out-of-range edit_n/unresolved_n and pass the standalone validator silently (documented conductor-side bounds check — is the split sound)?
7. Egress honesty: does the endorsement packet contain ONLY material already sent to these providers in rounds (check against the actual packet builder), and is the disclosure mention accurate?

## State
Suite: **1262 OK** (+53 over the 1209 post-P3 baseline), verified by an independent full run. Two finder agents already reviewed during implementation (the id-axis bug was their catch, fixed + regression-tested); this board review is the release gate.

